  "text": "Luke says she \"constrained us.\" She strong-armed the apostles into moving into her house. Acts sixteen. Paul and his companions get to Philippi. And on the sabbath they go out of the city by a river side, where prayer was made. They sit down and speak to the women who gathered there. And there is Lydia. Seller of purple. From Thyatira. Purple is the luxury trade. She has money and she runs a business. And the text says the Lord opened her heart, that she attended unto the things which were spoken of Paul. She is baptized. Her whole household with her. And then she makes an offer that is not really an offer. \"If ye have judged me to be faithful to the Lord, come into my house, and abide there.\" And Luke adds four words that tell you everything about how that went. \"And she constrained us.\" She talked them into it. That is the phrasing. She would not take no. And here is the receipt. Later in that same chapter, after Paul and Silas get out of prison, after an earthquake, after the jailer's whole household is baptized, where do they go? They entered into the house of Lydia. Her house was the base of operations. Not asked. Told.",
